      "type": "text",
      "text": "Analyze the attached multi-view images of a mechanical/CAD part (aim for\nfront, back, left, right, top, bottom, and one isometric view if available).\nProduce a single JSON file describing the part's structure. Follow these\nsteps in order:\n\n1. FEATURE IDENTIFICATION\n   List distinct functional features (mounting points, joints, interfaces)\n   and structural features (ribs, shells, spines, cavities) visible across\n   the views.\n\n2. SECTIONING\n   Divide the part into logical sections. For each\n   section, capture: a short name, its relative location on\n   the part, and a plain-language description of its geometry. Assign a unique RGB colour for each section.\n\n3. For each section, reason about the FUNCTIONAL INTENT, the PHYSICAL BEHAVIOR of the section to perform the function.\n\n4. Analyse the RELATIONSHIPs between each sections. Two types of RELATIONSHIPS should be considered.\n   First, functional relationships, with a list of example relationships provided:\n   transmits_load, locates_mating_part, seals_against, guides_motion, provides_clearance,\n   constrains_dof, mounts_to, distributes_stress.\n   Second, structural relationships, with a list of example relationships provided:\n   shares_face, adjacent_to, coaxial_with, attached_to, contained_in, supports,\n   blends_into, offset_from.\n\n5. MAJOR SURFACE INVENTORY\n   Within each section, identify the MAJOR surfaces that are visually\n   distinguishable across the views (distinct flat facets, distinct\n   curved/cylindrical patches, especially fillet bands and chamfers) — not a full\n   CAD B-rep face count. Assign each face a unique 4-digit code (0000, 0001, ...)\n   sequential across the whole part. Give each face a short name and one-line\n   description. Note this is not an exact topological face count (which would\n   typically be much higher due to small fillet/blend faces invisible in\n   shaded renders), but include as many faces as you can.\n\n6. ADJACENCY ORDERING\n   Within each section's surface list, order the surfaces so each is\n   physically adjacent to its neighbors in the list (a walk across the\n   section, not an arbitrary order). If a section contains disconnected\n   sub-groups (e.g. two separate tabs that don't touch), order each\n   sub-group internally.\n\n7. A STEP file analysis report is also provided as a .txt file which includes the information of all the faces of this B-rep model. Analyse the information of the step model and match the major faces you identified before with the corresponding step face, identify their unique id.\n\n8. For the faces that are not matched up with major faces but within the STEP analysis report list. Categorise them into sections where they most likely belong. List their B-rep id from the STEP analysis report as minor faces for each section.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT (JSON, model.json, no markdown fences, use this schema):\n{\n  \"part\": {\"name\": \"\", \"description\": \"\", \"source_views\": [], \"symmetry_note\": \"\"},\n  \"Section\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"\",\n      \"name\": \"\",\n      \"features included\": [],\n      \"function intent\": \"\",\n      \"physical behavior\": \"\",\n      \"geometry\": \"\",\n      \"location\": \"\",\n      \"colour\": {\"R\": 0, \"G\": 0, \"B\": 0},\n      \"major_faces\": [{\"face_code\": \"0000\", \"name\": \"\", \"Brep_id\": \"FACE 0\", \"description\": \"\"}],\n      \"minor_faces_Brep_id\": [\"FACE 1\", \"FACE 2\"],\n      \"relationships\": [\n        {\n          \"source id\": \"\",\n          \"target id\": \"\",\n          \"function relationship\": \"\",\n          \"structural relationship\": \"\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nLabeled multi-view images of the original part follow. The STEP B-rep analysis report follows after the images.\n"
